# C# style rules for Homework Central

When editing C# anywhere in this repository (`backend/`, `scripts/`, `tools/`):

- Do not use `var`. Always write explicit types for locals, foreach variables, and deconstruction
  targets. This also covers pattern positions — write the real type in `is` and `case` patterns
  rather than `is var x` / `case var x`. The one unavoidable exception is an anonymous type
  assigned to a local, which has no nameable type; prefer keeping anonymous types inline so the
  exception does not arise.
- Do not write the word `var` in prose either — comments, XML docs and string literals included.
  Write "implicitly typed local". The CI scan matches the bare word, which is what lets it stay
  simple and complete.
- Do not use `dynamic` for locals, fields, parameters, return types or type arguments. `var` is
  statically typed and merely inferred, whereas `dynamic` defers binding to runtime. Unlike `var`,
  "dynamic" *is* allowed in prose — the risk engine's "dynamic threshold" is a real domain term.
- Prefer pattern matching (`switch` expressions, property/relational patterns) over large `if` /
  `else if` chains for closed-set decisions such as intake types, scan statuses, and account
  classes.
- Match existing naming, primary constructors, and file-scoped namespaces in the project.
- Before adding or changing comments, XML docs, Markdown, naming, or function structure, read and
  follow the Comment Documentation Guide at
  [`docs/COMMENT_DOCUMENTATION_GUIDE.md`](../../docs/COMMENT_DOCUMENTATION_GUIDE.md).

- Never suppress the rule. No `#pragma warning disable IDE0008`, `<NoWarn>`, `[SuppressMessage]`,
  `eslint-disable`, nested `.editorconfig`, or nested `Directory.Build.props`/`.targets` — a nested
  MSBuild file shadows the root one and silently disables the check for that subtree. CI rejects
  all of these.

Enforced at build and in CI. The canonical statement of the rule, its exceptions, and which gate
owns which case live in [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) — do not restate the enforcement chain here,
because a second copy drifts.
